For each of the first three faulty BDT implementations required by Step
1.5, identify the function where there is a bug. You do not have to
identify the bug itself, only its location, but note that this must be
the location of the underlying error, which is not necessarily where
the error manifests itself or is "noticed" by the client.


      Executable         Function with the underlying error
===============================================================
         ./bdtBad1        BDT_InsertPath()
---------------------------------------------------------------
         ./bdtBad2        BDT_InsertPath()
---------------------------------------------------------------
         ./bdtBad3        BDT_rmPath()
===============================================================

For bdtBad4, you do not have to find the bug. Instead, indicate what
is different about trying to debug this executable in gdb versus the
other three:

The first three bad executables failed on specific function calls in 
the gdb. We could identify which specific functions had the error. 
This executable has an error that doesn't manifest until we print the
BDT. Debugging with gdb allows us to find which specific block of code
contains the bug and which function likely has issues (rmPath), but we 
cannot identify the specific line where it occurs. 

For bdtBad5, you do not have to find the bug. Instead, indicate one
hypothesis for how the program could crash within the DynArray module,
which we promise is itself correct:

The bug is located in BDT_toString(). Assuming that the implementation
is similar to that of dtGood, the BDT_toString function relies on three
DynArray functions: free, map, and new. We know these three functions
to be implemented correctly, and free and new do not rely on any 
information from BDT while map does. Therefore, we can infer that the 
bug occurs sometime during the calling of map().

When the client code calls map(), one of the parameters passed is a 
callback function. The assert failing here is within this callback. 
Since the client is controlling what happens in dynarray_map(), if they
do not pass dynarray correct parameters, then the function will fail. 
In particular, if the client does not write a callback function that 
aligns with how they have set up the nodes in the rest of their 
implementation, then when map() runs the callback function in these 
nodes, it will fail an assert. 

------------------------------------------------------------------------
What are your critiques of the given DT implementations, as
specified in Step 2.5?

First off there is a commented out function for Node_getPath in 
nodeGood.c. This is bad style and if we were to use this implementation
rather than the uncommented out implementation,
it would fail resource management as it does not free allocated memory 
(pathCopy) and does not include a comment in node.h mentioning 
that the caller is responsible for freeing it.

We can also see there is an issue of consistency. In node.h, the 
parameter order is not consistent in the functions 
Node_T Node_create(const char* dir, Node_T parent) and 
int Node_addChild(Node_T parent, const char* dir). Another quibble has 
to do with the parameter naming convention for 
int Node_compare(Node_T node1, Node_T node2). To remain consistent 
with the other function parameters, it would be better to rename the 
parameters as n1 and n2. 

There are also many functions in dtGood.c that are redundant or unnecessary. 
For instance, there is no need to have separate functions for 
DT_traversePathFrom and DT_traversePath. DT_traversePath is just a 
more specific version of DT_traversePathFrom. 

Similarly, there is no need for separate functions DT_rmPathAt and DT_rmPath. 
The functionality for DT_rmPathAt can just be placed inside DT_rmPath, 
considering that only 1 function, DT_rmPath uses DT_rmPathAt. 

Another function which seems unnecessary is DT_linkParentToChild. The caller 
can simply just call Node_linkChild and call Node_destroy on the child if 
the link is unsucessful. 

There are also a lot of asserts which are redundant because the inputs
don't change. For instance, in Node_linkChild it calls the 
assert(CheckerDT_Node_isValid(parent)) and 
assert(CheckerDT_Node_isValid(child)) multiple times in the conditionals
despite parent and child not changing at all. Furthermore, in the case of
the parameter (parent) not changing in the function, it would be better
to make the parameter const. 

Another critique is that the function Node_hasChild is a public function
despite it not being used outside of nodeGood.c. To prevent the client 
from understanding more of the uderlying structure of nodeGood.c it is best
that we make this a static function.

In nodeGood.c, Node_addChild essentially does the same job as Node_create 
and Node_linkChild in conjunction. As a result, it would be better to 
make Node_create and Node_linkChild be static functions, used internally in
Node_addChild. Or we can also choose to get rid Node_addChild and simply
have the caller use Node_create and Node_linkChild.

Lastly, there are very few local comments in both dtGood.c and nodeGood, 
which makes it very difficult to understand.
It is good that there are comments describing the static functions, however,
for clarity purposes it would be better to make comments describing the work 
performed in long functions like DT_insertRestOfPath.



